091156.947: [09:11:56][root@172-16-208-2 ~]# man ip
091157.244: [09:11:57]IP(8)                                                                                                  Linux                                                                                                 IP(8)
091157.245: [09:11:57]
091157.245: [09:11:57]NAME
091157.245: [09:11:57]       ip - show / manipulate routing, network devices, interfaces and tunnels
091157.245: [09:11:57]
091157.245: [09:11:57]SYNOPSIS
091157.245: [09:11:57]       ip [ OPTIONS ] OBJECT { COMMAND | help }
091157.245: [09:11:57]
091157.246: [09:11:57]       ip [ -force ] -batch filename
091157.246: [09:11:57]
091157.250: [09:11:57]       OBJECT := { link | address | addrlabel | route | rule | neigh | ntable | tunnel | tuntap | maddress | mroute | mrule | monitor | xfrm | netns | l2tp | tcp_metrics | token | macsec | vrf | mptcp }
091157.250: [09:11:57]
091157.255: [09:11:57]       OPTIONS := { -V[ersion] | -h[uman-readable] | -s[tatistics] | -d[etails] | -r[esolve] | -iec | -f[amily] { inet | inet6 | link } | -4 | -6 | -I | -D | -B | -0 | -l[oops] { maximum-addr-flush-attempts } |
091157.257: [09:11:57]               -o[neline] | -rc[vbuf] [size] | -t[imestamp] | -ts[hort] | -n[etns] name | -N[umeric] | -a[ll] | -c[olor] | -br[ief] | -j[son] | -p[retty] }
091157.257: [09:11:57]
091157.257: [09:11:57]OPTIONS
091157.257: [09:11:57]       -V, -Version
091157.257: [09:11:57]              Print the version of the ip utility and exit.
091157.257: [09:11:57]
091157.258: [09:11:57]       -h, -human, -human-readable
091157.258: [09:11:57]              output statistics with human readable values followed by suffix.
091157.258: [09:11:57]
091157.258: [09:11:57]       -b, -batch <FILENAME>
091157.258: [09:11:57]              Read commands from provided file or standard input and invoke them.  First failure will cause termination of ip.
091157.258: [09:11:57]
091157.259: [09:11:57]       -force Don't terminate ip on errors in batch mode.  If there were any errors during execution of the commands, the application return code will be non zero.
091157.270: [09:11:57]
091157.271: [09:11:57]       -s, -stats, -statistics
091157.271: [09:11:57]              Output more information. If the option appears twice or more, the amount of information increases.  As a rule, the information is statistics or some time values.
091157.271: [09:11:57]
091157.271: [09:11:57]       -d, -details
091157.271: [09:11:57]              Output more detailed information.
091157.271: [09:11:57]
091157.271: [09:11:57]       -l, -loops <COUNT>
091157.272: [09:11:57]              Specify maximum number of loops the 'ip address flush' logic will attempt before giving up. The default is 10.  Zero (0) means loop until all addresses are removed.
091157.272: [09:11:57]
091157.272: [09:11:57]       -f, -family <FAMILY>
091157.273: [09:11:57]              Specifies the protocol family to use. The protocol family identifier can be one of inet, inet6, bridge, mpls or link.  If this option is not present, the protocol family is guessed from other ar‐
091157.274: [09:11:57]              guments. If the rest of the command line does not give enough information to guess the family, ip falls back to the default one, usually inet or any.  link is a special family identifier meaning
091157.274: [09:11:57]              that no networking protocol is involved.
091157.274: [09:11:57]
091157.274: [09:11:57]       -4     shortcut for -family inet.
091157.274: [09:11:57]
091157.274: [09:11:57]       -6     shortcut for -family inet6.
091157.274: [09:11:57]
091157.274: [09:11:57]       -B     shortcut for -family bridge.
091157.274: [09:11:57]
091157.274: [09:11:57]       -M     shortcut for -family mpls.
091157.274: [09:11:57]
091157.275: [09:11:57]       -0     shortcut for -family link.
091157.275: [09:11:57]
091157.275: [09:11:57]       -o, -oneline
091157.275: [09:11:57]              output each record on a single line, replacing line feeds with the '\' character. This is convenient when you want to count records with wc(1) or to grep(1) the output.
091157.275: [09:11:57]
091157.275: [09:11:57]       -r, -resolve
091157.276: [09:11:57]              use the system's name resolver to print DNS names instead of host addresses.
091157.276: [09:11:57]
091157.276: [09:11:57]       -n, -netns <NETNS>
091157.276: [09:11:57]              switches ip to the specified network namespace NETNS.  Actually it just simplifies executing of:
091157.276: [09:11:57]
091157.276: [09:11:57]              ip netns exec NETNS ip [ OPTIONS ] OBJECT { COMMAND | help }
091157.276: [09:11:57]
091157.276: [09:11:57]              to
091157.276: [09:11:57]
091157.277: [09:11:57]              ip -n[etns] NETNS [ OPTIONS ] OBJECT { COMMAND | help }
091157.277: [09:11:57]
091157.277: [09:11:57]       -N, -Numeric
091157.277: [09:11:57]              Print the number of protocol, scope, dsfield, etc directly instead of converting it to human readable name.
091159.063: [09:11:59]
091159.317: [09:11:59]       -a, -all
091159.338: [09:11:59]              executes specified command over all objects, it depends if command supports this option.
091159.369: [09:11:59]
091159.370: [09:11:59]       -c[color][={always|auto|never}
091159.391: [09:11:59]              Configure color output. If parameter is omitted or always, color output is enabled regardless of stdout state. If parameter is auto, stdout is checked to be a terminal before enabling color out‐
091159.439: [09:11:59]              put. If parameter is never, color output is disabled. If specified multiple times, the last one takes precedence. This flag is ignored if -json is also given.
091159.930: [09:11:59]
091159.947: [09:11:59]              Used color palette can be influenced by COLORFGBG environment variable (see ENVIRONMENT).
091159.975: [09:11:59]
091159.989: [09:11:59]       -t, -timestamp
091200.004: [09:12:00]              display current time when using monitor option.
091200.044: [09:12:00]
091200.188: [09:12:00]       -ts, -tshort
091200.413: [09:12:00]              Like -timestamp, but use shorter format.
091200.438: [09:12:00]
091200.464: [09:12:00]       -rc, -rcvbuf<SIZE>
091200.465: [09:12:00]              Set the netlink socket receive buffer size, defaults to 1MB.
091200.489: [09:12:00]
091200.500: [09:12:00]       -iec   print human readable rates in IEC units (e.g. 1Ki = 1024).
091200.522: [09:12:00]
091200.561: [09:12:00]       -br, -brief
091200.571: [09:12:00]              Print only basic information in a tabular format for better readability. This option is currently only supported by ip addr show and ip link show commands.
091200.595: [09:12:00]
091200.596: [09:12:00]       -j, -json
091200.609: [09:12:00]              Output results in JavaScript Object Notation (JSON).
091200.622: [09:12:00]
091200.642: [09:12:00]       -p, -pretty
091200.664: [09:12:00]              The default JSON format is compact and more efficient to parse but hard for most users to read.  This flag adds indentation for readability.
091200.684: [09:12:00]
091200.706: [09:12:00]IP - COMMAND SYNTAX
091200.726: [09:12:00]   OBJECT
091200.743: [09:12:00]       address
091200.898: [09:12:00]              - protocol (IP or IPv6) address on a device.
091200.918: [09:12:00]
091200.937: [09:12:00]       addrlabel
091200.948: [09:12:00]              - label configuration for protocol address selection.
091200.949: [09:12:00]
091200.968: [09:12:00]       l2tp   - tunnel ethernet over IP (L2TPv3).
091200.969: [09:12:00]
091200.969: [09:12:00]       link   - network device.
091200.977: [09:12:00]
091200.995: [09:12:00]       maddress
091200.995: [09:12:00]              - multicast address.
091201.002: [09:12:01]
091201.012: [09:12:01]       monitor
091201.017: [09:12:01]              - watch for netlink messages.
091201.018: [09:12:01]
091201.027: [09:12:01]       mptcp  - manage MPTCP path manager.
091201.033: [09:12:01]
091201.041: [09:12:01]       mroute - multicast routing cache entry.
091201.046: [09:12:01]
091201.054: [09:12:01]       mrule  - rule in multicast routing policy database.
091201.059: [09:12:01]
091201.067: [09:12:01]       neighbour
091201.076: [09:12:01]              - manage ARP or NDISC cache entries.
091201.081: [09:12:01]
091201.090: [09:12:01]       netns  - manage network namespaces.
091201.095: [09:12:01]
091201.103: [09:12:01]       ntable - manage the neighbor cache's operation.
091201.115: [09:12:01]
091201.131: [09:12:01]       route  - routing table entry.
091201.144: [09:12:01]
091201.149: [09:12:01]       rule   - rule in routing policy database.
091202.702: [09:12:02]
091203.318: [09:12:03]       tcp_metrics/tcpmetrics
091203.334: [09:12:03]              - manage TCP Metrics
091203.350: [09:12:03]
091203.372: [09:12:03]       token  - manage tokenized interface identifiers.
091203.381: [09:12:03]
091203.396: [09:12:03]       tunnel - tunnel over IP.
091203.407: [09:12:03]
091203.414: [09:12:03]       tuntap - manage TUN/TAP devices.
091203.422: [09:12:03]
091203.423: [09:12:03]       vrf    - manage virtual routing and forwarding devices.
091203.431: [09:12:03]
091203.436: [09:12:03]       xfrm   - manage IPSec policies.
091203.445: [09:12:03]
091203.457: [09:12:03]       The names of all objects may be written in full or abbreviated form, for example address can be abbreviated as addr or just a.
091203.464: [09:12:03]
091203.478: [09:12:03]   COMMAND
091203.497: [09:12:03]       Specifies the action to perform on the object.  The set of possible actions depends on the object type.  As a rule, it is possible to add, delete and show (or list ) objects, but some objects do not al‐
091203.504: [09:12:03]       low all of these operations or have some additional commands. The help command is available for all objects. It prints out a list of available commands and argument syntax conventions.
091203.522: [09:12:03]
091203.536: [09:12:03]       If no command is given, some default command is assumed.  Usually it is list or, if the objects of this class cannot be listed, help.
091203.549: [09:12:03]
091203.556: [09:12:03]ENVIRONMENT
091203.566: [09:12:03]       COLORFGBG
091206.387: [09:12:06]              If set, it's value is used for detection whether background is dark or light and use contrast colors for it.
091206.455: [09:12:06]
091206.484: [09:12:06]              COLORFGBG environment variable usually contains either two or three values separated by semicolons; we want the last value in either case.  If this value is 0-6 or 8, chose colors suitable for
091206.510: [09:12:06]              dark background:
091206.560: [09:12:06]
091206.591: [09:12:06]              COLORFGBG=";0" ip -c a
091206.645: [09:12:06]
091206.741: [09:12:06]EXIT STATUS
091206.782: [09:12:06]       Exit status is 0 if command was successful, and 1 if there is a syntax error.  If an error was reported by the kernel exit status is 2.
091206.816: [09:12:06]
091206.851: [09:12:06]EXAMPLES
091206.895: [09:12:06]       ip addr
091206.950: [09:12:06]           Shows addresses assigned to all network interfaces.
091207.005: [09:12:07]
091207.157: [09:12:07]       ip neigh
091207.164: [09:12:07]           Shows the current neighbour table in kernel.
091207.196: [09:12:07]
091207.210: [09:12:07]       ip link set x up
091207.229: [09:12:07]           Bring up interface x.
091207.237: [09:12:07]
091207.244: [09:12:07]       ip link set x down
091207.251: [09:12:07]           Bring down interface x.
091207.257: [09:12:07]
091207.261: [09:12:07]       ip route
091207.276: [09:12:07]           Show table routes.
091207.281: [09:12:07]
091207.281: [09:12:07]HISTORY
091207.285: [09:12:07]       ip was written by Alexey N. Kuznetsov and added in Linux 2.2.
